   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the vehicle and traffic law and the public officers law, in\nrelation to establishing a school speed zone camera demonstration program in\nthe city of Yonkers; and providing for the repeal of such provisions upon\nexpiration thereof",
    "basePrintNo": "S10564",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S10564-2025",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Add §1180-i, V & T L; amd §87, Pub Off L",
    "lawSection": "Vehicle and Traffic Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S10564\n\nSPONSOR: STEWART-COUSINS\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the vehicle and traffic law and the public officers law,\nin relation to establishing a school speed zone camera demonstration\nprogram in the city of Yonkers; and providing for the repeal of such\nprovisions upon expiration thereof\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nThe purpose of this bill is to authorize the City of Yonkers to imple-\nment a pilot program for a speed violation camera monitoring system in\nschool speed zones located within the City.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 of the bill amends the Vehicle and Traffic Law to authorize\nthe City of Yonkers to operate a pilot program for a speed violation\ncamera monitoring system in no more than twenty-five school speed zones\nlocated in the City of Yonkers. The City is authorized to operate the\nspeed cameras (A) on school days beginning one hour before school hours\nand ending one hour after school hours; and (B) any day where a school\nis hosting student activities beginning thirty minutes before the school\nactivity and ending thirty minutes after the school activities. Speed\ndata, crash history and roadway geometry must all be considered by the\ncity when determining where to install and operate the devices. This\nsection also sets the conditions that a speed violation monitoring\nsystem must satisfy each day before it is used and annual calibration\nrequirements. Finally, section one requires that the City prepare a\nyearly report for the legislature and the executive on the pilot program\nfor the duration of the program.\n\nSection 2 amends the Public Officers law to ensure that any photographs,\nmicrophotographs, videotape, or other recorded images captured by a\nspeed camera in the City of Yonkers is exempt from any Freedom of Infor-\nmation Law requests.\n\nSection 3 states that the City must follow the appropriate public-con-\ntract-awarding process outlined in existing General Municipal Law when\npurchasing or leasing any equipment for its speed violation camera moni-\ntoring system pilot program.\n\nSection 4 is the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nDriving over the speed limit is a contributing factor in approximately\ntwenty-nine percent of traffic-related fatalities nationwide. The\ndangers of speeding are magnified when they occur in school zones, where\nlarge numbers of students arrive by bus or on foot and are frequently\npresent outdoors.\n\nFollowing the first implementation of a school speed zone camera pilot\nprogram in the State, New York City has seen average speed incidences\nreduced by seventy-three percent in school zones where speed cameras\nwere installed, and reductions of incidences up to ninety percent in\ncertain locations. Speed cameras have also been proven effective in\npreventing repeat offenses, as forty-six percent of drivers who received\na ticket did not receive a second violation. This success has demon-\nstrated how school speed zone camera programs can significantly improve\nstreet safety for students and surrounding communities.\n\nThe State Legislature has permitted the expansion of school speed zone\ncamera pilot programs outside of New York City, including neighboring\nWestchester County municipalities White Plains and Mount Vernon. The\nCity of Yonkers seeks authorization to operate a school speed zone\ncamera pilot program in its school zones to protect its students and\nincrease the safety of City streets.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nThis is a new bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone to the State.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect on the thirtieth day after it shall have\nbecome a law and shall expire December 31, 2031, when upon such date the\nprovisions of this act shall be deemed repealed. Effective immediately,\nthe addition, amendment and/or repeal of any rule or regulation neces-\nsary for the implementation of this act on its effective date are\nauthorized to be made and completed on or before such effective date.\n",
